A Frobenius series has the form

$
  y(x) = sum_(n=0)^oo a_n (x - x_0)^(n+sigma) = (x - x_0)^sigma sum_(n=0)^oo a_n (x - x_0)^n,
$

where $sigma$ can be real or complex and $a_0 != 0$, so that $sigma$ is unique. The series converges for some neighborhood of $x_0$. Unlike a power series about an ordinary point, it is not in general a Taylor series.

After multiplying the ODE by a suitable power of $x$, it looks equidimensional, so substituting the series extracts a common factor of $x^sigma$. The coefficient of the lowest power $x^sigma$, at $n = 0$, is the indicial equation; its roots are the possible values of $sigma$. Higher powers then give recurrence relations for the coefficients.
